1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:18,080
Fifty million miles from Earth, in the silent void of deep space, an alarm breaks the silence. A solar storm has erupted, and an astronaut’s vital signs are dropping.

2
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:29,050
There is no doctor on board, and Mission Control is twenty minutes away. We are Team Extokened from Bangladesh. I am Sajidul Islam Sobuj, lead software architect, alongside clinical researcher Yarin.

3
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:43,000
As humanity prepares to return to the Moon and journey to Mars, we created Storm Shield — an autonomous medical guardian engineered to protect astronaut health when Earth is out of reach.

4
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:59,330
Addressing NASA’s official challenge, 'Create Health Monitoring Software for Astronauts on Space Missions,' we examined the five foundational hazards of human spaceflight: radiation, isolation, distance, microgravity, and hostile closed environments.

5
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:19,760
On a Mars transit, radio delays reach twenty-two minutes, and solar conjunctions cause complete blackouts. Meanwhile, solar particle events surge proton flux over four-thousand-fold. Combined with microgravity fluid shifts that strain the heart, an acute medical crisis cannot wait for Earth.

6
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:38,420
Current space wearables stream vitals to ground scientists, but leave isolated crews without real-time guidance during blackouts. And black-box generative AI risks dangerous hallucinations. Astronauts need immediate, transparent, and grounded clinical intelligence.

7
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:59,740
This is Storm Shield. Our innovation links space weather directly to human physiology. We ingest live alerts from NASA CCMC DONKI and satellite telemetry from NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center GOES-16 Proton Flux, benchmarked against the NASA OSDR RadLab Database.

8
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:15,780
Applying Artemis I Orion Radiation Measurements, our engine models four-fold shelter protection. Wearable sensor feeds are evaluated by an interactive Health Twin against the astronaut’s personal baseline and NASA Human-System Standard 3001.

9
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:36,430
In this live demonstration, Pilot Alex Vance develops acute distress. Our persistence engine confirms the anomaly, alerts the crew across browser tabs, and opens Care Mode. Because Alex has an aspirin allergy, administration is strictly blocked, instantly creating a thirty-second flight surgeon handoff.

10
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:57,600
Who benefits? Astronauts and Crew Medical Officers gain an immediate, panic-free co-pilot. Crucially, Storm Shield operates at NASA Autonomy Ladder Level 2: the human acts, while the software visualizes, calculates, alerts, and records, with zero medical hallucinations.

11
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:18,010
What changes is total situational clarity. Our deterministic engine has passed forty-four automated unit tests with zero unanchored data. Beyond space, this offline architecture brings life-saving decision support to Antarctic research bases, naval vessels, and remote disaster zones on Earth.

12
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:28,040
Looking forward, we are advancing Storm Shield toward hardware Bluetooth biometrics and private on-device language models for ambient medical charting.

13
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:44,000
In deep space, autonomy is not a luxury — it is survival. We are Team Extokened. With Storm Shield, wherever humanity journeys, our astronauts will never face the storm alone.
